"time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-06-30","committee":"Policy and Resources Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-14","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Policy and Resources Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Worcester Council behind on five audit recommendations on spending controls","lead":"Worcester Council's Audit and Governance Committee was told the council has 19 outstanding internal audit recommendations, of which 5 are overdue. The overdue findings cover transparency code publication, reporting of purchasing card spending, and contract management monitoring. The council has implemented 74 per cent of recommendations overall, just below its 75 per cent target. The recommendations follow audits of the council's financial controls and procurement processes.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Appx 1 Internal Audit Progress Report WCC","paper_url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk/documents/s66110/66110.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Appx 1 Internal Audit Progress Report WCC","paper_url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk/documents/s66110/66110.pdf","headline":"Five audit recommendations are overdue across financial controls, procurement and transparency areas","detail":"The council has 19 outstanding audit recommendations, of which 5 are overdue. These cover Transparency Code publication requirements (2 findings), Purchasing Cards expenditure reporting (1 finding), and Contract Management KPI monitoring (2 findings). The council's overall recommendation implementation rate is 74%, just below the 75% target.","category":"governance","matters":"Overdue audit recommendations indicate the council is not promptly addressing identified control gaps. Delays in implementing recommendations on financial controls and procurement could affect the reliability of financial reporting and expose the council to operational risk.","figures":"5 of 19 outstanding recommendations overdue; implementation rate 74% against 75% target","quote":"There are 19 outstanding internal audit recommendations; however, and 5 of these are overdue.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Q1 Capital monitoring report 26-27","paper_url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk/documents/s66100/66100.pdf","headline":"Large \u00a36.957m crematorium refurbishment project proceeding on budget to October 2026 completion","detail":"Astwood Crematorium refurbishment and replacement cremators project is the largest single capital investment at \u00a36.957m total cost. Funded by \u00a31m Salix environmental grant with \u00a33.296m prudential borrowing in 2026/27. Project remains on budget and is expected to complete in October 2026, demonstrating successful delivery of major capital projects in contrast to delays and cost pressures on regeneration schemes.","category":"capital","matters":"Demonstrates council can deliver large capital projects on schedule and budget, providing positive signal on project management capability","figures":"Total cost \u00a36.957m, Salix grant \u00a31m, 2026/27 borrowing \u00a33.296m, Expected completion October 2026, Currently on budget","quote":"The total budget is \u00a36.957m and is funded from Salix Grant \u00a31m with the remainder being supported through prudential borrowing. The project is expected to be completed in October 2026 and is currently on budget.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Q1 Capital monitoring report 26-27","paper_url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk/documents/s66100/66100.pdf","headline":"LAHF housing acquisitions for temporary accommodation financed by borrowing dependent on rental income","detail":"Council is using Local Authority Housing Fund grant (\u00a3243.6k in 2026/27) matched with 50-60% council funding through prudential borrowing to purchase housing for temporary accommodation and refugee resettlement. Council has purchased 9 dwellings to date; 2 purchased this year using \u00a3165k grant and \u00a3184k borrowing. Financial sustainability depends entirely on rental income covering property repairs, maintenance, and debt service costs.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Yield-based borrowing model creates direct link between occupancy rates and debt serviceability; shortfalls in rental income would impair ability to service debt","figures":"LAHF grant 2026/27: \u00a3243.6k, Match funding through borrowing: 50-60% of grant, Recent purchases: 2 dwellings with \u00a3165k grant + \u00a3184k borrowing","quote":"The level of rent covers property repairs and maintenance and borrowing costs.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Q1 Capital monitoring report 26-27","paper_url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk/documents/s66100/66100.pdf","headline":"Prudential borrowing increased by \u00a34.1m to \u00a310.6m to finance expanded capital programme","detail":"Original approved borrowing of \u00a36.467m has increased to \u00a310.603m (71% increase) in the revised programme. Major borrowing allocations include \u00a33.296m for crematorium refurbishment, \u00a34.028m for Future High Streets projects, \u00a3710k for Barrington House temporary accommodation property, and \u00a3362k for housing acquisitions. Borrowing now finances 39% of the total \u00a327.3m capital programme.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Material increase in capital borrowing affects council's debt position and impacts future borrowing capacity","figures":"Approved borrowing \u00a36.467m, Revised borrowing \u00a310.603m, Increase \u00a34.081m","quote":"The project includes the refurbishment of Astwood Crematorium and replacement cremators. The total budget is \u00a36.957m and is funded from Salix Grant \u00a31m with the remainder being supported through prudential borrowing.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Q1 Capital monitoring report 26-27","paper_url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk/documents/s66100/66100.pdf","headline":"Scala Theatre and Corn Exchange projects showing \u00a3596k combined cost overruns and slipping to 2027","detail":"Future High Streets-funded Scala Theatre is not completing until early 2027, with forecast overspend of \u00a3224k against budget of \u00a34,943k. Corn Exchange Cultural Investment project has forecast overspend of \u00a3372k against budget of \u00a33,706k, with public realm works delayed into 2027. Council is requesting additional budget for these schemes in a separate agenda item. The combined overspend on these two flagship regeneration projects is the primary driver of overall capital forecast pressure.","category":"capital","matters":"Cost overruns and schedule delays on major regeneration projects indicate project management challenges and financing risks that could affect future capital capability","figures":"FHSF project: budget \u00a34,943k, forecast \u00a35,167k, variance \u00a3224k; Cultural project: budget \u00a33,706k, forecast \u00a34,078k, variance \u00a3372k","quote":"Forecast spend for 2026/27 is \u00a327.429m, and net pressure of \u00a30.163m mainly relating to additional costs of the Public Arts Venue at the Scala Theatre and Corn Exchange","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Worcester","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"Five audit recommendations are overdue across financial controls, procurement and transparency areas","lead":"Overdue audit recommendations indicate the council is not promptly addressing identified control gaps. Delays in implementing recommendations on financial controls and procurement could affect the reliability of financial reporting and expose the council\u2026","also":["17 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Policy and Resources Committee on 2026-11-17","n_findings":18,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appx 1 Internal Audit Progress Report WCC","top_paper_url":"https://committee.worcester.gov.uk/documents/s66110/66110.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":51,"news_drafts_total":1}}